# ANAS — AHR read-cache device removal (story ahrcache.1, AHR-DESIGN §13).
#
# Installed by the ANAS installer as /etc/udev/rules.d/99-anas-cache.rules.
# Inert on a node with no AHR read cache: nothing on the system carries a
# `<pool>-cacheN` GPT partition name until `POST /v1/ahr/<pool>/cache` writes
# one.
#
# WHY AN EVENT AND NOT A POLLER. dm-cache does not fall through to the origin
# (GT-19): the instant the cache device goes, EVERY read on the pool returns
# EIO within the second, promoted or not — files that were never promoted fail
# identically to cached ones, and a raw read of the LV itself fails. A poller's
# interval would be the length of the outage. udev knows in the same second the
# kernel does, and the recovery it triggers (`lvconvert --uncache`) restores
# read service on the very next I/O, in under a third of a second (GT-20).
#
# WHAT IT MATCHES. The partition NAME ANAS itself wrote into the GPT when the
# cache was attached — `<pool>-cache<n>`, which udev exposes as
# ID_PART_ENTRY_NAME. That label is the on-disk record of what the slice is
# for, and it is the same string `findCacheSlices()` reads back.
#
# WHAT IT MUST NOT MATCH, and cannot: an AHR BAND member slice is labelled
# `<pool>-d<n>-b<band>`, which carries no `-cache` segment, so a pulled pool
# disk never enters this rung. Band members are mdadm's business and reach ANAS
# through /usr/local/bin/anas-md-event (the --monitor PROGRAM hook) instead —
# md owns failover, this rung owns a layer md cannot see.
#
# The RUN program posts a one-line report to anasd over its root-only Unix
# socket and exits; the daemon answers 202 immediately and does the work in a
# job, so nothing here runs long enough to meet udev's event timeout.

ACTION=="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="partition", ENV{ID_PART_ENTRY_NAME}=="?*-cache[0-9]", RUN+="/usr/local/bin/anas-cache-event %E{ID_PART_ENTRY_NAME} %k"
ACTION=="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="partition", ENV{ID_PART_ENTRY_NAME}=="?*-cache[0-9][0-9]", RUN+="/usr/local/bin/anas-cache-event %E{ID_PART_ENTRY_NAME} %k"
